    {
        "content": "<p><code>Std</code> is not a package you can require, it's builtin in Lean, just <code>import Std</code>.</p>",
        "id": 588726764,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1776807041
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just to be clear: The advice given was to base your project off <code>leanprover/lean4-nightly:nightly-2026-04-22</code>. In a Lake project this amounts to writing that string into <code>lean-toolchain</code> which tells Lake which Lean version you are using.</p>\n<p>The easiest way to ensure that you are on the desired release is to create a new project using Lake</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Bash\"><pre><span></span><code>lake<span class=\"w\"> </span>+nightly-2026-04-22<span class=\"w\"> </span>new<span class=\"w\"> </span>my_new_nightly_project\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>If you edit a Lean module inside that project you can then do</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Std</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Std</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Http</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Body</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 590157984,
        "sender_full_name": "fiforeach",
        "timestamp": 1776851132
    }
]